WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.240
Happy to be here. I guess it's my first time in a long time giving a talk about something

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:09.360
that isn't about data science tools or data infrastructure, data systems, things like

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:16.160
that. Honestly, I mean, I was an AI skeptic for the longest time and didn't engage much

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:21.719
with these tools or systems. And, you know, suddenly coding agents burst onto the scene

00:00:21.719 --> 00:00:27.160
and I would say that I haven't really written code in about a year, or at least not meaningful

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:32.320
code. And so that's definitely put me into a contemplative mood thinking about the future

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:37.959
of software engineering and what role there is for people like me today who have a lot

00:00:37.959 --> 00:00:45.840
of experience building software and systems and what's going to be our role going forward.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:51.639
But in general, you know, it's clear that, you know, we as developers are already writing

00:00:51.639 --> 00:00:57.400
a lot less code. I essentially don't write code anymore. I think that's going to become

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:03.400
increasingly the norm for a large category of systems. There may still be certain specialized

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:10.080
systems, databases, things that require deep expertise and the agents don't know a lot

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.360
about where they're still going to be written by human hand. I expect that the DuckDB developers

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:18.919
are going to continue to write code by hand for a lot of things. But a lot of classes

00:01:18.919 --> 00:01:25.000
of software aren't going to be written by people. And so that begs the question, what

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:32.879
is left for us to do? And the argument that I, you know, I will pose in this talk, and

00:01:32.879 --> 00:01:36.879
I don't know that I will convince you of everything that I'm going to say, mainly my goal is to

00:01:36.879 --> 00:01:42.680
provoke ideas and dialogue about these things, is that these three kind of human traits of

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:47.879
understanding the scope of what you're building, how to design it, how to architect it, and

00:01:47.879 --> 00:01:55.320
taste what makes it good are really the most important things that are left. But I will

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.760
also argue that these were also the things that mattered the most in the first place.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:06.199
And that coding was this annoying human labor that got in the way of actually building the

00:02:06.199 --> 00:02:10.960
things. But if you didn't have these essential ingredients, it was very difficult to build

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:16.520
a piece of high quality production software. And there are many pitfalls along the way

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:22.320
that could result in creating software that fails to ship or falls apart under the weight

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:27.559
of its own bad decisions. And let's face it, we use a lot of software products every day

00:02:27.559 --> 00:02:32.160
that simply aren't very good. I'm going to talk about that as well and what we're going

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.759
to do about it. You know, there are people in the software craftsmanship community that

00:02:36.759 --> 00:02:42.160
have a lot spicier takes than I do. I followed Uncle Bob for going on 20 years since I read

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:47.720
his book Clean Code in the late 2000s. And he responded to Brian Armstrong's recent tweet

00:02:47.720 --> 00:02:53.679
about cutting head count as a result of AI and having non-technical people shipping production

00:02:53.679 --> 00:02:57.440
code. And he said, honestly, I don't think he knows what he's talking about. What the

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:02.320
agents give us is the ability to disengage from deep syntax, but they do not give us

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:06.479
the ability to disengage from modular design and architecture. You still need to be deeply

00:03:06.479 --> 00:03:13.639
technical in these topics in order to produce good quality production code. I will say I

00:03:13.639 --> 00:03:18.759
largely agree with this with some nuances. And so I'm going to wade into these topics

00:03:18.759 --> 00:03:23.559
before I later in the talk, I'll move on and talk about some of the systems and tools that

00:03:23.559 --> 00:03:28.000
I've been building for myself to fully embrace agentic engineering and to feel more comfortable

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:36.279
about using agents to produce production code. So if you've never heard about Fred Brooks,

00:03:36.679 --> 00:03:44.839
he was at IBM and led one of the largest software projects of the 20th century, building OS360,

00:03:44.839 --> 00:03:50.880
IBM mainframes. This led him to write the Mythical Man-Month in 1975, which you say,

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:58.800
wow, 1975 is a long time ago, 51 years. Still is enduringly one of the most cited and most

00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:04.279
influential works on software management and software engineering in history. And even

00:04:04.279 --> 00:04:07.639
today you're starting to see, you know, I wrote an essay about the Mythical Man-Month,

00:04:07.639 --> 00:04:13.479
like Martin Fowler just wrote an essay about it. It is still influencing minds and it is

00:04:13.479 --> 00:04:22.239
coming up more and more in today's context of agents carrying an increasingly large burden

00:04:22.239 --> 00:04:28.640
of the software that we're writing. He wrote a follow-up essay to the Mythical Man-Month that

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:33.440
was also very influential called No Silver Bullet, which introduces, I think, a helpful

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:38.959
framework in thinking about software complexity that I will come back to and explain.

00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:44.079
But if you've never read this book, and I admit that I have never actually read this book cover

00:04:44.079 --> 00:04:52.480
to cover. I've mainly read excerpts and sections and quotes and ideas, but a lot of these ideas,

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:57.839
some of which have been lost and have to be relearned over and over again. But, you know,

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:02.720
Brooks's Law, the idea that adding manpower to a late software project makes it later,

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:07.119
it makes it later. It's one of those things that, you know, people forget and then they

00:05:07.119 --> 00:05:11.200
have to relearn the hard way. I don't know if any of you have been in a company where a pointy

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:15.200
haired boss says, this project is behind, we need to hire engineers to make it go faster.

00:05:16.079 --> 00:05:19.839
I'm usually the person in the room who's saying, wait, wait, like, have you read the Mythical

00:05:19.839 --> 00:05:23.359
Man-Month? Like, are you sure that this is a good idea? Maybe the problem is actually that

00:05:23.359 --> 00:05:26.720
you have the wrong people working on the project, not that you need to add people.

00:05:27.279 --> 00:05:34.239
And so this idea of the surgical team that a small team of elite people outperform large teams

00:05:34.239 --> 00:05:39.679
of average ones is also one of the reasons why startups can deliver innovations and disrupt

00:05:39.679 --> 00:05:46.239
industries in, you know, short amounts of time and run circles around their much larger and more

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:53.279
well-equipped competitors. One of the reasons that adding people to a software project

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.440
can make it go slower is because of the communication overhead, which grows

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:02.239
quadratically with the number of people. And so, you know, a 20 person software,

00:06:02.239 --> 00:06:07.440
20 person engineering team versus a five person engineering team, there's just a lot more overhead

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:11.920
in, you know, delegating work, splitting up tasks, going through design, getting everybody on the

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:17.679
same page. You need to introduce layers of management to keep people on track. And, you know,

00:06:17.679 --> 00:06:24.799
the whole, you know, agile practice was created to try to mitigate some of these issues. And, you

00:06:24.799 --> 00:06:29.600
know, I would argue that the practice of agile is practically dead today and any team that's still

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:36.640
having backlog grooming and sprint planning is doing it wrong. Another idea that comes from this

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:44.880
book is the idea that the difference between a working software, you know, project and a software

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:50.320
product that you can sell and can be maintained in production is roughly like an order of magnitude

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:55.040
in the amount of work. And, you know, now when we have agents producing, we have agents producing

00:06:55.040 --> 00:07:02.399
software and it's alluringly simple to sit down with cloud code, build a brand new piece of

00:07:02.399 --> 00:07:08.000
software and say, oh, looks good, I tested it, it works, like, let's ship it. And it's kind of

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:14.880
missing kind of that layer of work, which is described in this 9X gap that would be involved

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:20.399
in, you know, actually running that project in production over a long period of time and all the

00:07:20.399 --> 00:07:27.359
problems that can come up along the way. And so, you know, so a lot of these ideas are summed up

00:07:27.359 --> 00:07:31.279
in thinking about software architecture and software design is this notion of conceptual

00:07:31.279 --> 00:07:37.440
integrity. And so it's the idea that when you look at a software project, you want it to seem

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:43.200
like it was designed by one mind. So like one kind of coherent set of ideas, even if there were many

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:48.799
people implementing and developing it. And so if you have a project that has a bunch of

00:07:48.799 --> 00:07:56.559
disparate or like discordant ideas, then, you know, that can create a lot of problems as the project

00:07:57.119 --> 00:08:02.880
scales. And so Brooks says, you know, if the project reflects one set of design ideas, it's

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:08.000
better than having many good but independent and uncoordinated ideas. And of course, nowadays we

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:12.640
have agents jumping in and adding lots of independent and uncoordinated ideas to all of our

00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:22.640
software projects. So in the no silver bullet essay in 1986, Brooks introduced this idea of two

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:30.239
kinds of software complexity, the essential complexity, which is basically what the system

00:08:30.239 --> 00:08:35.679
solves, how it needs to be designed in order to be a working solution for what you're trying to

00:08:35.679 --> 00:08:41.520
build. And that involves the conceptual integrity and having a coherent picture of like these are

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:46.320
the essential set of features for this to be a complete system. If we take anything out, the

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:53.440
project is no longer a solution for what we're trying to solve. And then accidental complexity

00:08:53.440 --> 00:08:58.640
is everything else that is introduced by our tools and processes and technologies that we're using

00:08:59.359 --> 00:09:03.359
to build the system. And so that involves our programming languages, our build tools,

00:09:03.359 --> 00:09:09.200
like all of our systems that do testing and QA and continuous integration, you know,

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:13.679
the boilerplate that's involved with interacting with databases and all that fun stuff.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:21.919
And so agents are tremendously powerful tools for dealing with all of this accidental complexity

00:09:21.919 --> 00:09:28.000
that comes up while you're building a software project. You can say, you know, hey, Claude, hey,

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:34.559
Codex, you know, hey, Gemini sometimes, go and refactor, you know, all of this code, change the

00:09:34.559 --> 00:09:41.520
test framework, rewrite all of the tests. It's happy to dive in and attack and deal with all

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.200
of this complexity that is introduced by, you know, the tools and systems that you're using

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:52.719
to build the project. And I'm encountering this firsthand myself as I've largely moved on to use

00:09:52.719 --> 00:09:56.799
other programming languages other than Python, which I will show you in more graphic detail in

00:09:56.799 --> 00:10:02.159
a little while. I've never written a line of Go professionally or a line of Swift professionally.

00:10:03.039 --> 00:10:07.520
And yet I'm, you know, in any given day, I might be slinging, you know, 5 or 10 or 20,000 lines of

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:11.919
code in these programming languages. I'm not an expert in, like, what are the right tools and

00:10:11.919 --> 00:10:16.960
frameworks and libraries that I should be using in each language. But, you know, I do look at the

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:23.119
code and see whether, like, is there a lot of code duplication, code smells, classic refactoring

00:10:23.119 --> 00:10:29.760
signals. And to be able to tell an agent, like, there's code duplication in this file or these

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:34.640
tests, like, seems like this test suite could benefit from test fixtures. And to be able to

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:39.919
work at that high level of being able to speak in the language of refactoring and clean code

00:10:39.919 --> 00:10:45.440
and to be able to go and reformulate whole parts of a software project in small amounts of time

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:50.000
has given me, like, a tremendous sense of agency. And what used to be, like, I'd have to pick and

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:54.559
choose my battles of, like, do I really want to spend today refactoring this test suite? Usually

00:10:54.559 --> 00:10:59.919
the answer is no. But now I can have, you know, the agents refactor my test suite while I'm having

00:10:59.919 --> 00:11:06.719
lunch. And that's a nice thing. The downside, of course, is that when the agents are working on,

00:11:06.719 --> 00:11:13.679
you know, the essential parts of the system, they have a difficult time distinguishing between

00:11:13.679 --> 00:11:20.719
the essential complexity and the accidental complexity. And they also generate new kinds

00:11:20.719 --> 00:11:28.080
of accidental complexity in doing the work that you need them to do. And so this could take the

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:33.840
form of, you know, creating the right, having the wrong domain model, like, the right, you know, the

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:38.479
wrong layering of classes or, like, failing to recognize that, you know, if you're building

00:11:38.479 --> 00:11:43.280
something UI related that you have to explain to the agent, like, we're using, you know, this is the

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:47.919
model, this is the view, this is the controller. But if you don't explain that to them and articulate,

00:11:47.919 --> 00:11:52.640
like, this is how the system should be designed, the default output in a lot of cases is just going

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:59.840
to be a hairball that becomes, you know, increasingly difficult for the agents to refactor.

00:12:01.359 --> 00:12:06.000
And this gets worse and worse as time goes on because fresh agent sessions come into the existing

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:11.200
code base and they're just as happy to, you know, bolt another appendage onto the Frankenstein

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:15.599
rather than taking a step back and saying, okay, what is this code, how should this code really be

00:12:15.599 --> 00:12:20.719
designed? You know, what types of, what are the right abstractions, like, the right layering of

00:12:20.719 --> 00:12:26.320
components to make this easy to test and maintainable, easy to refactor, easy to think

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:32.640
about. So it's very difficult because, you know, the agents, they're, they've been, you know,

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:37.440
reinforcement learned and reward hacked to just want to solve the problem that you put in front

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:42.880
of them. And so if you need, if you want them to clean up and improve the quality of the code base,

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:46.960
generally you have to ask them to do that explicitly. They won't just do it on their own.

00:12:47.599 --> 00:12:53.599
Another closely related idea to this that I, you know, I have to mention is the,

00:12:54.880 --> 00:13:01.359
you know, different, different software engineering person, but Conway's law is the idea that the

00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:08.400
organization, the software projects resemble the organizational structures that produce them.

00:13:08.400 --> 00:13:14.479
And so, you know, this is also one of the reasons why small teams often outperform large teams

00:13:14.640 --> 00:13:20.239
outperform large teams, because you have like a smaller group of people designing the whole system

00:13:20.239 --> 00:13:24.239
thinking in a coherent manner. Whereas when you say, okay, this person is responsible for the

00:13:24.239 --> 00:13:30.719
data plane, this, this, this you know, this team is responsible for the UI, this, you know, team

00:13:30.719 --> 00:13:35.359
for some other part of the system, you end up having like a lot of, a lot of negotiation and

00:13:35.359 --> 00:13:40.880
a lot of discordance and kind of the interactions between, between those systems. And that makes,

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:45.119
generally makes the software projects a great deal more complex and hard, hard to work with.

00:13:51.119 --> 00:13:56.400
Another, another issue that I've seen is that I think a lot of people are experiencing the joy

00:13:56.400 --> 00:14:02.559
of coding agents in the context of building greenfield software. So I don't know how many,

00:14:03.200 --> 00:14:08.880
for me, it's like actually a great joy to sit down and make dir and get in it and launch a

00:14:08.880 --> 00:14:14.799
fresh cloud code session and start and start building something. And you're creating files,

00:14:14.799 --> 00:14:19.679
you're creating build systems, you know, you're, you're terraforming kind of in a godlike manner,

00:14:19.679 --> 00:14:25.280
you know, with, with a wave of your hand. But that's a lot different from taking agents and

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:30.559
dropping them into an existing 3 million line production code base, which has been worked on by

00:14:30.559 --> 00:14:37.359
a hundred people over a period of 10 or 15 years. And we've seen, you know, anecdotally that, you

00:14:37.359 --> 00:14:43.280
know, agents struggle with these, with these large brownfield projects. But if you start a brand

00:14:43.280 --> 00:14:48.239
new project with coding agents, and despite your best efforts, eventually there's going to come a

00:14:48.239 --> 00:14:54.080
point where your lovely, delightful greenfield project is going to turn into a brownfield

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:59.919
project itself after, you know, a thousand agent sessions and something like that. And I don't know

00:14:59.919 --> 00:15:05.520
exactly where that, like, where that crossover point occurs, my sense is that it's somewhere in

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:10.640
the, somewhere in the low hundreds of thousands of lines of code. Like I noticed that, you know,

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:15.840
just the feeling of getting things done in a 200,000 line fully agent developed code base,

00:15:15.840 --> 00:15:19.840
it's just a lot different from like a 30,000 or 40,000 line code base. And a lot of that

00:15:19.840 --> 00:15:25.520
has naturally to do with, you know, the agent context and just the overall complexity of the

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:31.760
system. And you can introduce, you know, memory, you know, memory layers and design documents and

00:15:31.760 --> 00:15:36.080
to help provide affordances for the agents to be able to at least navigate the code base and spend

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:40.559
a lot of time calling tools and figuring out, like, what code should they be looking at? Like, what do

00:15:40.559 --> 00:15:45.520
they need to understand to be able to do things? But still, it's like, just with the architecture

00:15:45.520 --> 00:15:50.080
of LLMs, it's just fundamentally a problem. Like, there's kind of come a point where the code base

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:55.440
is big enough that the agents are going to start to have a difficult time doing what you need them

00:15:55.440 --> 00:16:02.960
to do. And so, I would describe this as the, in Mythical Man Month, it's described for the human

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:08.799
aspect, the tar pit. So, it's the idea that you can see the beast struggling in and it looks as if

00:16:08.799 --> 00:16:15.679
any of them could easily free themselves, but the tar holds them all together. And so, here, you

00:16:15.679 --> 00:16:21.359
know, we might have, you know, at any given time in a project, you might have five different coding

00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:26.960
agent sessions going on in different Git work trees. And they just, they don't really know,

00:16:26.960 --> 00:16:30.000
you know, have a great deal of understanding about what, you know, each of the different

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:35.200
agents are doing and the decisions that are making. And so, you often end up with, like,

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:40.799
complex rebases and having to, like, redo or, like, rethink work because, you know, the agents are all

00:16:40.799 --> 00:16:44.479
trampling on each other as you're trying to work in parallel and get things done. And it very much

00:16:44.479 --> 00:16:50.799
resembles the same kinds of problems that occur in the human space of, you know, individual humans

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:58.239
you know, typing on keyboards and working on, you know, pull requests in parallel. And so,

00:16:58.239 --> 00:17:03.200
you know, in this sense, you know, I do believe that we're, you know, continuing to accrue,

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:06.800
you know, a similar but slightly different type of technical debt, but we're doing it at machine

00:17:06.800 --> 00:17:13.839
speed. And so, we're starting to encounter the kinds of system scalability, development scalability

00:17:13.839 --> 00:17:18.719
problems that used to occur much later in the lifespan of a project. So, you could be starting

00:17:18.719 --> 00:17:25.040
to have these issues after working on a project for only a week or two rather than, like, a year

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:29.760
or two. And so, that's just a new problem that we have to we just have to reckon with.

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:38.079
So, if you think about, you know, how cheap and easy it is to generate code,

00:17:39.839 --> 00:17:45.359
it's easy to, you know, wake up every morning at, you know, 530 or 6 o'clock in the morning

00:17:45.359 --> 00:17:49.199
and while you're making coffee to drop your first prompt and ask, you know, ask one of

00:17:49.199 --> 00:17:55.280
the agents to build something. And the agents are happy to oblige. They, like, saying no is, like,

00:17:55.280 --> 00:17:58.959
almost not in their, I think it's not in their system prompts. Like, they're not allowed to say

00:17:58.959 --> 00:18:04.560
no, you know, unless you ask them to do something dangerous or illegal. And so, you know, if you ask

00:18:04.560 --> 00:18:09.760
them to build something for you, it's, you know, relatively rare that they're going to push back

00:18:09.760 --> 00:18:13.119
and say, like, no, that's not a good idea or, like, that's going to introduce, you know, too

00:18:13.119 --> 00:18:18.560
much complexity or are you sure that, like, this is the direction that you want to go. And so,

00:18:19.199 --> 00:18:24.319
essentially, you know, we are having, you know, we're turning into, you know, a lot of us were

00:18:24.319 --> 00:18:29.760
individual contributors, you know, working with a tech lead or a software architect. But now we

00:18:29.760 --> 00:18:35.599
are essentially all turning into architects or even product managers where our primary role is

00:18:35.599 --> 00:18:41.040
saying no. And when the agent proposes to do something, we have to be able to recognize that

00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:47.359
and say, no, no, pump the brakes. Like, I do not want you to do it. You do it like that. And if

00:18:47.359 --> 00:18:55.439
you don't, well, the bad decisions will pile up and you're back to the tar pit. This begs the

00:18:55.439 --> 00:19:06.640
question, how do you hold the agents accountable? It's hard. And you can certainly use agents to,

00:19:07.119 --> 00:19:13.280
you know, do adversarial review and design and architecture review. But I think the human in

00:19:13.280 --> 00:19:18.319
the loop still matters a lot. And I think this goes back to, you know, the point that Uncle Bob

00:19:18.319 --> 00:19:27.520
was making in Uncle Bob's way that if you lack the technical expertise to recognize when the

00:19:27.520 --> 00:19:31.839
agents have gone off the rails or have gone in a bad direction that's introducing a lot of unwanted

00:19:31.839 --> 00:19:38.079
or unreasonable complexity in your system, you know, you're going to end up in just a giant

00:19:38.079 --> 00:19:44.160
mess. And right now there's lots of non-technical or not very technical people who are using coding

00:19:44.160 --> 00:19:48.560
agents to build software who are all going to be learning this the hard way and wondering why

00:19:48.560 --> 00:19:54.319
they're having such a difficult time just making the software work. A lot of it will be found out

00:19:54.319 --> 00:19:58.719
in production, I'm sure, when they're getting complaints from their users when things are broken.

00:19:58.719 --> 00:20:08.160
And so, certainly, I believe there's, you know, a shift in the skills that matter now. Like,

00:20:08.160 --> 00:20:14.319
I spent, you know, over 20 years writing code professionally, really priding myself on my

00:20:14.319 --> 00:20:18.800
ability to write code, you know, my ability. I'm an Emacs user. So, like, I got really good at all

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:24.640
of the, like, key chords and, like, you know, being able to refactor code and navigate large

00:20:24.640 --> 00:20:30.160
code bases really quickly and type and write the code. And now I essentially don't have to do that

00:20:30.160 --> 00:20:36.479
anymore. And there's, but I do think that, you know, your ability to, like, build conceptual

00:20:36.479 --> 00:20:41.040
integrity, visualize the problems that you're solving and what the shape of the solution looks

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:44.800
like, you know, is one of the most important skills that you can build. And I think that

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:49.359
software engineers, especially computer science students, are going to have to spend a lot more

00:20:49.359 --> 00:20:54.880
time building this skill in a way that doesn't involve writing code. So, that's going to be,

00:20:54.880 --> 00:20:59.520
you know, reading more code, like, looking at the architecture and design of systems so that

00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:04.079
you even have the language to be able to express what you want to the agents.

00:21:06.479 --> 00:21:10.400
Certainly, that, you know, the next skill, like, being able to explain and communicate with the

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:18.640
agents, if you can't, you know, clearly articulate how you want the work to be done, it's, you know,

00:21:18.640 --> 00:21:23.760
agents will fill in the gaps with, you know, their own ideas about the way that things

00:21:23.760 --> 00:21:29.280
should be done. But it may not be right. And if you can't recognize that, you could spend a lot

00:21:29.280 --> 00:21:34.880
of time building a specification that creates, you know, creates things the wrong way or in a

00:21:34.880 --> 00:21:42.079
suboptimal way. And so, I do think that increasingly, like, we're going to be engaging primarily with

00:21:42.079 --> 00:21:48.000
specifications and less with the details of how to build things. And so, you know, and I'll talk a

00:21:48.000 --> 00:21:53.359
little bit about this in a few minutes. But it's going to be a lot more than sitting down and

00:21:53.359 --> 00:21:57.439
saying, you know, add a button, you know, add a button to this page. It could be more of, like,

00:21:57.439 --> 00:22:03.599
okay, let's have a broader discussion about, you know, the big picture goals of what this software

00:22:03.599 --> 00:22:08.239
does and, you know, its list of features, its non-features, so that you can build a detailed

00:22:08.239 --> 00:22:13.199
specification, which can be then adversarially reviewed and turned into an implementation plan

00:22:13.199 --> 00:22:17.760
and then implemented by a fleet of implementer agents and reviewers and all those things.

00:22:17.760 --> 00:22:22.880
Essentially the kind of stuff that the factory fellow was just talking about in his last talk.

00:22:24.959 --> 00:22:31.199
So, this all being said, the models are getting better. They're a lot better than they were six

00:22:31.199 --> 00:22:40.719
months ago. And six months before that was a big shift. So, you know, it's possible that these

00:22:41.680 --> 00:22:44.560
issues may go away as the harnesses get better and the models get better.

00:22:45.839 --> 00:22:52.319
But I'm not sure. And I do think that from what I've seen, engineers who have deep technical

00:22:52.319 --> 00:22:57.119
background and experience designing and building software are the ones currently who are getting

00:22:57.119 --> 00:23:02.400
the most leverage out of coding agents and who are producing the highest quality software with them.

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:06.959
And so, that gives me some confidence that, you know, the agents getting better at conducting

00:23:06.959 --> 00:23:12.239
software engineering tasks and implementing correct code, I'm sure that, you know, the code

00:23:12.239 --> 00:23:16.479
that they're writing will get better and better and more and more bug free. But I struggle to

00:23:16.479 --> 00:23:20.640
believe that they're going to solve these fundamental software design and scope and

00:23:20.640 --> 00:23:27.199
taste problems, which have always been, you know, the devilishly difficult part where humans are

00:23:27.199 --> 00:23:34.079
needed to make things that are good. So, I'll change gears a little bit and

00:23:35.040 --> 00:23:39.199
talk about some related ideas that I've had to this. And I'll talk a little bit about my personal

00:23:39.199 --> 00:23:44.719
agentic engineering stack. But there's a lot of software out there that isn't very good.

00:23:45.599 --> 00:23:49.599
Probably a lot of it you use every day and it frustrates you. I know that it frustrates me.

00:23:50.479 --> 00:23:55.520
Gmail.com frustrates me. Like, you know, most software products, as a builder of software,

00:23:55.520 --> 00:23:59.680
whenever I encounter something that seems not good, I'm often asking myself, like,

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:04.079
what product manager thought that this was good, that this was okay to ship in this state?

00:24:05.280 --> 00:24:14.400
And so, I do think that there is, you know, for individuals who have, you know, good design skills

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:21.199
and good taste, there is a tremendous opportunity to go and recognize, like, this old software,

00:24:21.199 --> 00:24:26.000
which is created pre-AI, is bad. Let's make something good. And I would challenge everyone

00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:30.319
in the room, there's really nothing stopping you from doing this. And so, if you encounter

00:24:30.319 --> 00:24:35.839
software products in your life that, you know, they're not good, you don't have to accept them

00:24:35.839 --> 00:24:41.119
the way that they are. And I believe it's, you know, kind of incumbent on all of us to

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:48.239
kind of embrace that challenge and be willing to reject the, you know, the status quo or at least

00:24:48.239 --> 00:24:55.439
the status quo pre-2026 and undertake work to build software projects that are good or at least

00:24:55.439 --> 00:25:02.479
better than the ones that we have. And because it used to be that, you know, the excuse was that

00:25:02.479 --> 00:25:07.439
it's hard to build things. Like, well, we would make it better, but we would have to spend a lot

00:25:07.439 --> 00:25:13.359
of money on engineers to write code to make it good. But now that that excuse is gone, that

00:25:13.359 --> 00:25:19.839
generating the code is free, the only excuses left are that you don't know what is good. Like,

00:25:19.839 --> 00:25:24.239
you don't know, you know, you don't know what's the right thing to build, and you can't recognize

00:25:24.239 --> 00:25:29.520
when it's good. And I think it's generally some combination of both. So, kind of back to the,

00:25:29.520 --> 00:25:33.760
you know, the product manager who thought, you know, the current state of Gmail search is acceptable.

00:25:35.839 --> 00:25:41.839
And so, you know, myself, like, I've, you know, embraced this completely and, you know, about six

00:25:41.839 --> 00:25:46.800
months ago, I went down the rabbit hole and it's like, okay, you know, it's time to build. Like, I

00:25:46.800 --> 00:25:51.839
spent a lot of 2025 in an existential crisis about, like, my role and, like, is my career

00:25:51.839 --> 00:25:56.560
as a software engineer over? Are the agents just going to be doing everything without me in the

00:25:56.560 --> 00:26:01.760
loop? And, you know, by about October, November, I was looking around, I'm like, agents are pretty

00:26:01.760 --> 00:26:07.280
good, and yet the software is not getting better very quickly, you know, very quickly. I should do

00:26:07.280 --> 00:26:14.000
something about that. And so, I've been, you know, been working, you know, day and night, you know,

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:19.760
largely seven days a week, maybe not the most healthy lifestyle generating code across a bunch

00:26:19.760 --> 00:26:23.760
of programming languages. One thing that's interesting is you can see a discrete point

00:26:23.760 --> 00:26:30.479
at the end of January where I became a former Python programmer. And so, you can see that delta

00:26:30.479 --> 00:26:36.079
at the end of January where I made up my mind to essentially delete almost all of my Python code

00:26:36.079 --> 00:26:43.359
and port it to Go. And so, I now largely consider myself an agentic Go programmer. But I want to

00:26:43.359 --> 00:26:49.359
talk about, you know, some of what my experience has been like going down this rabbit hole and

00:26:49.359 --> 00:26:52.880
some of the tools and systems that I'm building to try to

00:26:53.798 --> 00:26:58.798
and systems that I'm building to try to give me,

00:26:58.798 --> 00:27:03.598
essentially retain my agency where I am able to conduct

00:27:03.598 --> 00:27:08.998
a almost factory-like software process of building specifications,

00:27:08.998 --> 00:27:13.718
implementing those, doing quality verification similar to the factory talk.

00:27:13.718 --> 00:27:17.318
This also has influenced my choice of programming languages,

00:27:17.318 --> 00:27:19.958
and so I wrote an essay earlier this year talking

00:27:19.958 --> 00:27:22.798
about human ergonomics and agent ergonomics.

00:27:22.798 --> 00:27:25.998
One of the reasons why we use Python today is

00:27:25.998 --> 00:27:29.597
because it was an amazing programming language for humans to read and write,

00:27:29.597 --> 00:27:32.557
and why I got so motivated about building Pandas was like,

00:27:32.557 --> 00:27:37.318
wow, Python is an amazing programming language if only it could do data well.

00:27:37.318 --> 00:27:42.757
Fortunately, that ended up being a good bet and a good way to spend my time for many years.

00:27:42.757 --> 00:27:47.998
But now, agents don't care as much about whether Go is annoying to write or not.

00:27:47.998 --> 00:27:51.678
They care about whether they can compile and test really fast.

00:27:51.678 --> 00:27:54.557
There's an aspect of distributing the software.

00:27:54.557 --> 00:27:57.118
Do you have to carry around a virtual machine?

00:27:57.118 --> 00:27:59.838
How do you install your dependency stack?

00:27:59.838 --> 00:28:02.958
Being able to build a static binary and just

00:28:02.958 --> 00:28:05.838
CP it onto a server on your tail scale network,

00:28:05.838 --> 00:28:08.838
build it in three seconds and deploy it in four seconds,

00:28:08.838 --> 00:28:12.677
is a really nice feature of the language.

00:28:12.677 --> 00:28:15.997
These old ideas of human ergonomics,

00:28:15.997 --> 00:28:19.997
readability and the ease of writing code and working with code,

00:28:19.997 --> 00:28:22.717
and your text editor or your IDE,

00:28:22.717 --> 00:28:25.037
they just don't matter as much anymore.

00:28:25.037 --> 00:28:29.878
Some of the runtime infrastructure layer running our code,

00:28:29.878 --> 00:28:34.878
the JVM is even more of a burden today than it was a few years ago.

00:28:34.878 --> 00:28:38.437
You can obviously have the agents install the JVM and all that,

00:28:38.437 --> 00:28:40.437
but static binaries are really nice.

00:28:40.437 --> 00:28:44.757
I think that languages like Go and Rust that produce static binaries

00:28:44.757 --> 00:28:50.838
are prime beneficiaries of this new agent ergonomics.

00:28:51.077 --> 00:28:58.397
I also think that because of the fungibility of programming languages,

00:28:58.397 --> 00:29:03.158
and frameworks, and the top part of the application stack,

00:29:03.158 --> 00:29:07.678
that this also is causing more value to be pushed down the stack.

00:29:07.678 --> 00:29:11.518
The things that are lower in the stack are the things that are still hard to build.

00:29:11.518 --> 00:29:14.637
I struggle to believe that agents could be set to work and

00:29:14.637 --> 00:29:16.158
could rebuild DuckDB.

00:29:16.158 --> 00:29:17.837
Maybe somebody will do it someday,

00:29:17.837 --> 00:29:19.437
but we're not close to that,

00:29:19.437 --> 00:29:22.158
and those developers are still writing code by hand.

00:29:22.158 --> 00:29:24.717
Hannes told me himself.

00:29:24.717 --> 00:29:28.118
This is interesting in the sense that it does make

00:29:28.118 --> 00:29:32.077
all of these fundamental systems that power applications even more valuable,

00:29:32.077 --> 00:29:35.398
whereas the language user interface layer

00:29:35.398 --> 00:29:37.518
is increasingly fungible and doesn't matter as much,

00:29:37.518 --> 00:29:40.197
because agents can tear it down and build something new.

00:29:40.197 --> 00:29:41.958
You want to change the programming language?

00:29:41.958 --> 00:29:44.318
Fine, let's do that.

00:29:44.917 --> 00:29:49.758
I don't even describe the way that I work now as vibe coding,

00:29:49.758 --> 00:29:53.598
because I feel like vibe coding has become this pejorative,

00:29:53.598 --> 00:29:56.357
like, yeah, I don't write the code,

00:29:56.357 --> 00:29:57.557
I don't read the code either.

00:29:57.557 --> 00:29:59.958
But that's not the way that I work.

00:29:59.958 --> 00:30:03.637
Jesse Vincent, who created superpowers,

00:30:03.637 --> 00:30:06.197
he gave me permission to reuse this quote,

00:30:06.197 --> 00:30:08.277
which is from a private text thread.

00:30:08.277 --> 00:30:10.118
But the difference between vibe coding and

00:30:10.118 --> 00:30:12.158
agentic engineering is planning, architecture,

00:30:12.158 --> 00:30:13.637
and caring about the output.

00:30:13.637 --> 00:30:16.477
I'll tell you a little bit about what I've been doing

00:30:16.477 --> 00:30:20.837
in my token maxing adventures.

00:30:20.837 --> 00:30:25.437
I've burned something like a billion tokens per day the last 30 days.

00:30:25.437 --> 00:30:27.557
I'm a little bit slower since I'm here at the conference,

00:30:27.557 --> 00:30:29.318
conference Wi-Fi and things like that.

00:30:29.318 --> 00:30:32.557
But I am a superpowers user,

00:30:32.557 --> 00:30:34.917
and if you don't use superpowers,

00:30:34.917 --> 00:30:36.837
which is I think the most popular

00:30:36.837 --> 00:30:39.798
spec-driven development skills framework out there,

00:30:39.798 --> 00:30:42.118
I highly encourage you to check it out.

00:30:42.118 --> 00:30:43.958
It's how I get everything done now.

00:30:43.958 --> 00:30:48.637
But around superpowers and spec-driven development,

00:30:48.637 --> 00:30:53.357
I've been building a collection of open-source projects to try to

00:30:53.357 --> 00:30:58.318
bring sanity to my world of agentic engineering.

00:30:58.318 --> 00:31:01.038
These buckets are code quality assurance,

00:31:01.038 --> 00:31:04.118
session intelligence, development, visibility,

00:31:04.118 --> 00:31:07.357
observability, and project management.

00:31:08.197 --> 00:31:11.037
There's many skills frameworks.

00:31:11.037 --> 00:31:12.798
This one is good.

00:31:12.798 --> 00:31:18.757
I think the idea is that it brings discipline to your planning process.

00:31:18.757 --> 00:31:21.718
So first, clarify what you want to build,

00:31:21.718 --> 00:31:23.477
answer a lot of questions about the design.

00:31:23.477 --> 00:31:27.317
It presents the design in clear stages where you can read this.

00:31:27.317 --> 00:31:28.757
Human, make a decision.

00:31:28.757 --> 00:31:30.718
Is this the right design? That's your opportunity.

00:31:30.718 --> 00:31:34.877
That's where you can add your value to make the right design decisions

00:31:34.877 --> 00:31:38.718
because the spec will then be created based on those design decisions.

00:31:38.718 --> 00:31:40.357
When the spec is created,

00:31:40.357 --> 00:31:41.877
you can review the spec,

00:31:41.877 --> 00:31:43.838
you can have other agents review the spec.

00:31:43.838 --> 00:31:46.357
But eventually, you convert that into an implementation plan

00:31:46.357 --> 00:31:48.518
which takes a long time to implement.

00:31:48.518 --> 00:31:53.398
So one of the values of superpowers that allows you to

00:31:53.398 --> 00:31:56.357
specify and plan a lot of work

00:31:56.357 --> 00:31:59.718
upfront in these spec development design review sessions,

00:31:59.718 --> 00:32:01.838
and then you can kick off

00:32:01.838 --> 00:32:04.877
the sub-agent driven development and go take a walk or something.

00:32:04.877 --> 00:32:08.877
I actually had a superpowers plan that took 14 hours to implement.

00:32:08.877 --> 00:32:12.438
So sometimes, I find myself late at night,

00:32:12.438 --> 00:32:15.077
I got to get my plan implementing before I go to sleep.

00:32:15.077 --> 00:32:18.438
So I have something to look at in the morning.

00:32:18.438 --> 00:32:21.678
But using these frameworks,

00:32:21.678 --> 00:32:23.678
if you use them as is,

00:32:23.678 --> 00:32:31.077
they do reviews that assert spec conformance and to an extent code quality.

00:32:31.077 --> 00:32:35.278
But there's still a lot of bugs and problems in the generated code.

00:32:35.718 --> 00:32:39.877
In the factory talk,

00:32:39.877 --> 00:32:42.597
he's talking about Claude being not a good code reviewer.

00:32:42.597 --> 00:32:47.998
I agree. The GPT-5 line of models is

00:32:47.998 --> 00:32:51.558
a much better code reviewer, code validator.

00:32:51.558 --> 00:32:54.798
So I found myself in the beginning of January asking

00:32:54.798 --> 00:32:58.238
CodeX to review every commit that Claude was generating.

00:32:58.238 --> 00:32:59.917
After a minute, I was like,

00:32:59.917 --> 00:33:01.197
this should just happen automatically.

00:33:01.197 --> 00:33:02.477
Why do I even have to ask?

00:33:02.477 --> 00:33:05.317
So that was how RoboRev was born.

00:33:05.317 --> 00:33:09.958
So now, I have agents generating code,

00:33:09.958 --> 00:33:11.597
and my mantra is like every line of

00:33:11.597 --> 00:33:13.678
generated code gets reviewed at least once,

00:33:13.678 --> 00:33:17.597
usually four or five times before it ever lands into a production code base.

00:33:17.597 --> 00:33:20.998
This is a system that I built to make all of that possible.

00:33:20.998 --> 00:33:24.477
So somebody from the community said,

00:33:24.477 --> 00:33:28.477
catches issues that I would have shipped otherwise.

00:33:28.477 --> 00:33:32.357
I tend to rush things and this project keeps me honest.

00:33:32.357 --> 00:33:34.958
So one of the core concepts is that rather than

00:33:34.958 --> 00:33:38.637
reviews happening in the ephemeral agent session,

00:33:38.637 --> 00:33:42.838
the reviews all go into a ledger and they have to be explicitly closed.

00:33:42.838 --> 00:33:47.118
So it could be an agent closes the review or you close it yourself.

00:33:47.118 --> 00:33:49.798
But you don't want to do a code review and for

00:33:49.798 --> 00:33:53.118
the output of that code review to be lost like tears in the rain.

00:33:53.118 --> 00:33:54.917
So you want to explicitly say like,

00:33:54.917 --> 00:33:58.357
I read this either you or the agent looked at this code review,

00:33:58.357 --> 00:33:59.877
you addressed its feedback,

00:33:59.877 --> 00:34:01.558
and then you closed out the review.

00:34:01.558 --> 00:34:03.877
So now four months later,

00:34:03.877 --> 00:34:07.877
I've gone through something like 20,000 code reviews and RoboRef.

00:34:07.877 --> 00:34:13.077
So it truly is like a continuous autonomous code review system.

00:34:13.077 --> 00:34:20.637
I also found that it was frustrating like losing track of all my agent sessions,

00:34:20.637 --> 00:34:25.197
and there are a lot of half-baked versions of agents view that were built.

00:34:25.197 --> 00:34:30.357
So I wanted to create a unified session database for every coding agent,

00:34:30.357 --> 00:34:34.838
full-text search, analytics about tool use,

00:34:34.838 --> 00:34:37.518
token spend, where am I spending?

00:34:37.518 --> 00:34:38.678
If I were paying API rates,

00:34:38.678 --> 00:34:40.197
where would I be spending money?

00:34:40.197 --> 00:34:41.857
There's a bunch of these projects.

00:34:41.857 --> 00:34:43.357
I think this one's pretty good.

00:34:43.357 --> 00:34:46.637
So if you're interested in this and it doesn't have something you want,

00:34:46.637 --> 00:34:48.037
please come and drop a pull request.

00:34:48.037 --> 00:34:50.398
I will review your pull request.

00:34:50.398 --> 00:34:53.037
I did have the misfortune of

00:34:53.037 --> 00:34:55.637
deciding that I needed to build my own issue tracker after

00:34:55.637 --> 00:34:59.637
having beads delete half of my code base three or four times.

00:34:59.637 --> 00:35:02.477
So I tried a bunch of

00:35:02.477 --> 00:35:06.757
the lightweight local issue trackers and didn't find anything that I liked.

00:35:06.757 --> 00:35:08.037
So a couple of weeks ago,

00:35:08.037 --> 00:35:09.398
I started building Kata,

00:35:09.398 --> 00:35:10.998
which I encourage you to check out.

00:35:10.998 --> 00:35:12.477
It's just a SQLite database.

00:35:12.477 --> 00:35:15.477
There are some stuff in it for issue federation,

00:35:15.477 --> 00:35:21.958
but it's very helpful for agents to be able to have issues,

00:35:21.958 --> 00:35:23.958
where they can leave comments and keep track of

00:35:23.958 --> 00:35:27.597
the state of understanding that issue as they're working on it.

00:35:27.597 --> 00:35:31.517
So it's another place where agents can record things aside from

00:35:31.517 --> 00:35:34.477
a collection of scattered and disorganized markdown documents,

00:35:34.477 --> 00:35:36.438
which is their default.

00:35:37.597 --> 00:35:41.798
As we all know, GitHub has been falling apart.

00:35:41.798 --> 00:35:43.878
Barely works half the days.

00:35:43.878 --> 00:35:45.878
After a while, I just stopped going to GitHub

00:35:45.878 --> 00:35:49.517
because you click on the pull request tab.

00:35:49.517 --> 00:35:51.277
It says there's 27 pull requests,

00:35:51.277 --> 00:35:52.517
but there's only two showing them.

00:35:52.517 --> 00:35:55.998
So it's like, how do you even get work done on this platform?

00:35:55.998 --> 00:36:01.438
So I decided to basically build a new UI for GitHub.

00:36:01.438 --> 00:36:03.517
It uses the GitHub UI.

00:36:03.517 --> 00:36:05.878
So now this is, I don't go to github.com anymore.

00:36:05.878 --> 00:36:09.878
I use Middleman for keeping track of all my projects,

00:36:09.878 --> 00:36:12.277
all the activity, I merge pull requests,

00:36:12.277 --> 00:36:15.517
I comment on issues, I look at code reviews.

00:36:15.517 --> 00:36:18.717
We're building some other stuff in this project.

00:36:18.717 --> 00:36:20.878
So if GitHub is frustrating you,

00:36:20.878 --> 00:36:22.157
this is what I use.

00:36:22.758 --> 00:36:25.318
I'm sure a lot of people are going to be building

00:36:25.318 --> 00:36:30.798
their own slightly different version of this to support their workflow.

00:36:30.798 --> 00:36:35.037
I've tried out a bunch of the parallel agent managers.

00:36:35.037 --> 00:36:36.998
I do currently use Superset,

00:36:36.998 --> 00:36:40.558
not Apache Superset, but superset.sh.

00:36:40.558 --> 00:36:42.397
It's got a lot of bugs.

00:36:42.397 --> 00:36:44.037
If anyone in the audience is from Superset,

00:36:44.037 --> 00:36:48.517
please fix the bugs that I've reported, please.

00:36:48.517 --> 00:36:50.078
I can put up a pull request,

00:36:50.078 --> 00:36:52.277
but you won't merge it, so it's fine.

00:36:52.277 --> 00:36:53.957
Just fix the bugs.

00:36:53.957 --> 00:36:56.638
But I do a lot of work in this system,

00:36:56.638 --> 00:36:59.837
and so at any given time,

00:36:59.837 --> 00:37:02.998
I might have 20 or 30 get work trees on my machine,

00:37:02.998 --> 00:37:05.597
and especially when I'm dealing with inbound pull requests,

00:37:05.597 --> 00:37:07.917
to be able to go from pull request number,

00:37:07.917 --> 00:37:10.238
to get work tree, to hey Claude,

00:37:10.238 --> 00:37:12.837
run RoboRev and fix what's wrong with this PR,

00:37:12.837 --> 00:37:14.477
and then I landed in Middleman.

00:37:14.477 --> 00:37:19.558
So that's how my process works in collaborative development.

00:37:20.318 --> 00:37:21.998
So I'm running right up on time,

00:37:21.998 --> 00:37:23.558
and we've got a few minutes for questions,

00:37:23.558 --> 00:37:27.638
but I hope to provoke some thoughts

00:37:27.638 --> 00:37:29.558
about the future of software engineering,

00:37:29.558 --> 00:37:34.118
and I fully embraced this after being a skeptic

00:37:34.118 --> 00:37:37.157
for the longest time, and I'm having a lot of fun,

00:37:37.157 --> 00:37:39.037
and I think the fact that we get to build tools

00:37:39.037 --> 00:37:41.438
while we're doing it to figure out how to do this better

00:37:41.438 --> 00:37:44.717
makes it even more fun as a long time tool builder.

00:37:44.717 --> 00:37:47.037
So thanks for listening,

00:37:47.037 --> 00:37:48.998
and look forward to your questions.

00:37:50.078 --> 00:37:51.037
Thank you.

